dwm - dynamic window manager
============================
dwm is an extremely fast, small, and dynamic window manager for X.

This is a modified dwm. Windows float by default and are arranged with a task
view, half screen snapping and side by side snapping instead of switchable
layouts. Windows can also be moved and resized from the keyboard, and moved by
holding both mouse buttons. See dwm.1 (man dwm) for the full list of key and
mouse bindings.


Requirements
------------
In order to build dwm you need the header files for Xlib, Xft, Xinerama,
XInput2, fontconfig and dbus. On distributions that package headers separately
these are the -dev or -devel variants, for example:

    Arch            libx11 libxft libxinerama libxi fontconfig dbus
    Debian, Ubuntu  libx11-dev libxft-dev libxinerama-dev libxi-dev libdbus-1-dev

XInput2 is needed for the two mouse button move gesture only. Comment out
XINPUT2LIBS and XINPUT2FLAGS in config.mk to build without it: dwm then prints
a warning at startup, that one gesture is disabled, and everything else works
as before.


Themes (no recompile, no restart)
---------------------------------
Colors are runtime-switchable. Themes are read from ~/.config/edwm/themes/*.conf
(and *.theme); the colors in config.h are the always-present built-in default.

    MODKEY-n            next theme
    MODKEY-Shift-n      previous theme

A theme file names colors in a [palette] and maps them onto scheme slots in a
[scheme] section; every slot row is "fg[, bg[, border]]" and each part is a
palette name or a literal color. Unlisted slots keep the built-in defaults
(tagsel falls back to sel, tasksel to sel, status to norm). A flat
"norm_fg = #hex" layout also works. dmenu follows the theme via dmenu_nb and
friends.

    # ~/.config/edwm/themes/example.conf
    [palette]
    bg      = #282828
    fg      = #EBDBB2
    accent  = #458588
    urgent  = #CC241D
    [scheme]
    sel      = fg, accent, accent
    tasksel  = fg, accent, accent
    status   = fg, bg

Theme switches are also possible over D-Bus (see below). Switching only swaps
already-parsed colors: a handful of Xft allocations, a border repaint and one
bar redraw per monitor; there is no per-frame cost and no flicker. A theme with
an unparseable color is refused with a warning, the old colors stay.


D-Bus
-----
At startup dwm makes sure a session bus exists: it uses DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
when set, then $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus, and otherwise starts its own dbus-daemon.
The address is exported to every child dwm spawns, and
dbus-update-activation-environment is run (best effort) so bus-activated
services see DISPLAY, so notifications and tray apps work without dbus-launch.

dwm also owns the org.edwm name on the session bus:

    busctl --user call org.edwm /org/edwm org.edwm.Theme List
    busctl --user call org.edwm /org/edwm org.edwm.Theme Current
    busctl --user call org.edwm /org/edwm org.edwm.Theme Next
    busctl --user call org.edwm /org/edwm org.edwm.Theme SetTheme s nord

Every switch (keybinding or bus) emits the signal org.edwm.Theme.ThemeChanged
with the new theme name. If the bus dies dwm keeps running and retries the
connection every few seconds.


Bar
---
The middle of the bar shows one button per window on the current tags; the
focused window is highlighted, urgent windows are inverted and staylow
(minimized) windows are dimmed. Buttons that do not fit shrink and truncate.

    Button1 on a button    focus (on the focused button: staylow minimize)
    Button2 on a button    zoom
    Button3 on a button    toggle that window's staylow state

The right end of the primary monitor's bar hosts the system tray; tray apps
dock their icons there automatically (XEmbed). Clicks on tray icons go to
their apps.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dwm is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install dwm (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running dwm
-----------
Add the following line to your .xinitrc to start dwm using startx:

    exec dwm

In order to connect dwm to a specific display, make sure that
the DISPLAY environment variable is set correctly, e.g.:

    DISPLAY=foo.bar:1 exec dwm

(This will start dwm on display :1 of the host foo.bar.)

In order to display status info in the bar, you can do something
like this in your .xinitrc:

    while xsetroot -name "`date` `uptime | sed 's/.*,//'`"
    do
    	sleep 1
    done &
    exec dwm


Configuration
-------------
The configuration of dwm is done by editing config.h and (re)compiling the
source code.

The Makefile only copies config.def.h to config.h when config.h does not exist
yet, and this tree ships both, so edits have to go into config.h to take
effect. Apply them to config.def.h as well to keep the two in step.
